      "whole_MAIN": {
        "bytes": 8919,
        "SHA256": "4f306ac0820292296c4b90fa09f01fa57428519a11df7b201711b33a5220928a"
      },
      "abstract_parts": 3,
      "full_abstract_in_JSONLD_and_DC": true,
      "explicit_math_serialization": "For every finite positive sum g(z)=∑_{j}λ_{j}(z−z_{j})^(-2) with ∑_{j}λ_{j}=1, we construct a compact set S(g) of area exactly π such that ∫_{{|z|<R}∖S(g)}|g(z)| dA ≤ 2π log R+π(log 2+e^(-1)) (R>1). The same set serves every radius, with no restriction on the poles. This gives an affirmative deduction for Baernstein's question, Hayman–Lingham Problem 7.37, from the established Eremenko–Hamilton indicator estimate and Levine–Peres quadrature-set construction. The logarithmic coefficient is sharp; no optimality is claimed for the additive constant. Historical first priority is not certified. AI tools were used extensively; this note is unrefereed.",
      "native_assets_and_Bib_exact": true,
      "canonical": "https://aleckriebel.com/research/fixed-area-squared-pole-sums/"
